WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.140 align:start position:0%
 
Wow  hmm  despite the Yakuza series cult status  mainstream success has always eluded it  in the West if you've never played a  Yakuza game before however Yakuza zero  is a logical place to test the waters  it's the series debut on PlayStation 4  and has a prequel of the first Yakuza  game it doesn't rely on pre-existing  knowledge of its principal characters  more importantly you should play zero  because it's a fascinating game that  combines equal parts drama and comedy  and is unlike anything else after such a  statement is worth scrutinizing so to be  clear its zeros flaws that we've out to  you at first glance be it some seriously  outdated character models and textures  or the repetitive nature of combat but  were some elements languished from a  lack of attention to detail other facets  of zero are masterfully executed take  the story for example which jumps back  and forth from the perspective of two  different Yakuza on opposite sides of  Japan kiri u Kazuma is a young Yakuza  gangster from Tokyo with an iron fist  but a heart of gold and he's caught in  the middle of a battle between criminal  organizations seeking to take control of  a valuable piece of real estate on the  other side of the country in Osaka we  meet goro majima a disfigured Yakuza  masquerading as the manager of a grand  cabaret also on the outs with his clan  Majin was sent on a mission to kill a  troublesome business owner but soon  finds himself unable to complete the job  for personal reasons zero takes its  storytelling seriously nobody cracks  jokes or makes empty threats as such the  story is relentlessly tense proving to  be equal parts surprising and exciting  from the beginning to the end  [Music]  you come to here  [Music]  while the game is only playable in  Japanese with English subtitles the  energy and attitude behind most  characters doesn't need to be translated  bosses or more appropriately captains  are often rendered with photorealistic  facial features as well some textures go  too far revealing what looks like  extreme cases of clogged pores but  blemishes aside zeroes key characters  look just as convincing as they sound  navigate in the wha-whoa a genome to  continue almost across the board however  zeros other characters exhibit middling  animations most NPCs look as though they  were lifted from the series PlayStation  3 entries if not from a PlayStation 2  Yakuza key given that moving through the  story missions is only half of the  Aquajet experience this is a reality you  have to confront on a regular basis  Hanahan combat is another key component  of zero that feels dated despite any  improvements made upper past games both  cure you and majima feature different  fighting styles three varieties of peace  but zero straightforward beat-em-up  trappings ultimately grow repetitive by  and large you can choose one fighting  style that works for you and focus on  that for the entire game the enemies you  face on the street are somewhat diverse  and include the likes of lonesome drunks  bikers and lowly Yakuza thugs  thematically the variety is appreciated  but mechanically most enemies fight the  same  at least zero is nothing if not a  brutally violent game you will grind  enemy's faces into the pavement stick a  bat in their mouth and kick the exposed  end and piledrive thugs skull first into  the street and special takedowns like  these add a necessary amount of flair to  combat saving fights from becoming truly  Road more you play the more apparent it  becomes that zero wants you to feel both  like a badass Yakuza and like a  participant in an absurdist comedy no  one will argue that a Yakuza on the run  has time to pretend to be a random  girl's boyfriend to impress her father  or stand-in as a producer on a TV  commercial but the game's 100 ridiculous  side stories are excellent palate  cleansers that often elicit a chuckle  make you scratch your head and amusement  and refresh your perspective traditional  for the series zero also doesn't shy  away from thrusting you into erotic  situations be it in the form of softcore  porn video parlors or in a minigame that  involves betting on wrestling matches  between two scantily clad women at best  you can momentarily excuse its  quote-unquote tasteless pursuits as a  reflection of Japanese society in the  late 80's or accept them at face value  as erotica stereotypically however  Yakuza view women as objects to be  manipulated and this issue can't be  avoided if zero aims to present a  realistic Yakuza tale just don't expect  the game to address it in a meaningful  way otherwise zero relentlessly adheres  to his Japanese roots mostly for the  better and if you've ever traveled to  Japan the game sights and sounds will  instantly trigger fond memories and  feelings of nostalgia so unless you have  a strong aversion to violence sex or  middle and graphics you really owe it to  yourself to give zero chance its story  will surprise you its inhabitants will  make you laugh at every turn and its  ambitious scope will redefine how you  think about open-world games it's a  fascinating adventure no matter how you  approach it and proof positive that a  game can be wildly inconsistent yet  remain a great experience  [Music]  you  [Music]

